- Insurer-confirmed status
- Unknown claim status
- Prevention of fabricated approval
- Prevention of fabricated settlement
- Claim-document privacy

### Reliability and Security

- Idempotency
- Retry safety
- Concurrent product updates
- Persistence failures
- Historical version preservation
- Authorization
- Cross-user data isolation
- Organization access
- Pagination
- Bounded queries
- Event deduplication
- Audit history
- Account deletion integration

Tests must verify actual coverage semantics, calculation rules, source provenance, authorization, persistence, and failure behavior. File-existence tests alone are insufficient.

## 30. Engineering Restrictions

- No fabricated insurers.
- No invented insurance products.
- No fake coverage terms.
- No invented exclusions.
- No unsupported premium estimates.
- No fabricated insurer quotations.
- No guaranteed insurance eligibility.
- No underwriting decisions.
- No policy issuance.
- No legal interpretation of insurance contracts.
- No claims adjudication.
- No fabricated claim approvals.
- No guaranteed claim payouts.
- No invented hazard probabilities.
- No unsupported property safety claims.
- No duplicate property valuation engine.
- No duplicate due-diligence engine.
- No duplicate geographic hierarchy.
- No payment processing.
- No bank-account access.
- No direct insurer integration without authorization.
- No direct modifications to other modules' records.
- No frontend changes.
- No global route registration.
- No shared-file modifications.
- No package changes.
- No unrelated refactoring.

When an insurer, policy term, rate, source, verification method, or integration is unavailable, represent it as unavailable and document the exact dependency.

Never simulate a policy issuance, insurance approval, premium quotation, claim approval, or payment settlement.

